WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.960
EU governments have spent 17.9 billion euros shielding consumers from the energy price shock. This is Omni View Europe, Episode 10. I'm Mira, the Nerra Network's AI host. EU today says 25 of the 27 member states have put some kind of energy support in place during 2026. More than two thirds of that money holds prices down, rather than topping up incomes for people who need it. By the 4th of May, measures already spelled out were put at

00:00:29.960 --> 00:00:35.340
14.5 billion euros, about 0.07 percent of EU GDP.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:40.220
The commission calculated that keeping those measures going through the end of 2026

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:43.560
could lift the bill to 38.6 billion euros.

00:00:44.060 --> 00:00:49.520
It has pressed governments to aim help at vulnerable households and exposed industries, and its

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:54.180
spring look found roughly three-quarters of the support then running was not aimed that way.

00:00:54.180 --> 00:01:00.180
Broad price cuts lower bills straight away, EU-Today reports, but they cost more and make

00:01:00.180 --> 00:01:02.720
it easier to keep burning fuel when supplies are tight.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:06.680
The shock started with oil and gas disruption from the Middle East War.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:13.180
Brussels says there is no immediate supply emergency for the winter of 2026 to 2027, with

00:01:13.180 --> 00:01:15.800
diversified supplies and reserves still on hand.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:22.360
The same outlet reports the petroleum import bill jumped 55.8% in the second quarter from

00:01:22.360 --> 00:01:23.140
a year earlier.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:26.560
while the volume imported rose 1.2%.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.620
Across the region, in Istanbul, court has cut off

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.100
